new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f228569
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,145 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on a single server
+
+[`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) deploys the standalone version of Fizzy
+with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/). One application container runs Rails,
+SQLite, Solid Queue background jobs (including recurring jobs), Solid Cache, and
+Solid Cable. Uploads are stored locally. Kamal Proxy runs alongside it and obtains
+and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for HTTPS. No MySQL, Redis, S3, or separate
+job server is needed.
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server with Docker installed and SSH access. Kamal uses `root` by
+  default; set `ssh.user` in the deploy config if you use another user with Docker
+  access.
+- A hostname whose DNS points directly at that server. Ports 80 and 443 must be
+  publicly reachable for the proxy and certificate issuance; SSH must be reachable
+  from your deployment machine. Don't run another web server on ports 80 or 443.
+- An SMTP service that the server can reach, and a sender address authorized by
+  that service. Fizzy sends sign-in codes by email, so working mail is essential.
+- On your development/deployment machine: a fork or clone of this repository,
+  Ruby from `.ruby-version`, Bundler, and Docker with Buildx. Run `bundle install`.
+  Use the standalone Gemfile: don't enable `SAAS` or create `tmp/saas.txt`.
+
+Run the commands below from the repository root on your deployment machine, not
+on the server. `bin/kamal` uses the version locked in this repository;
+`bundle exec kamal` is equivalent.
+
+## Configure
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+   - Replace `fizzy.example.com` under `servers.web` with your public hostname
+     (without `https://` or a path). The YAML anchor also sets `proxy.host` and
+     `APP_FQDN`, so HTTPS, email links, and URLs generated by jobs agree.
+   - Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_ADDRESS`, and your provider's SMTP settings.
+     The defaults use authenticated SMTP on port 587 with STARTTLS. See the mail
+     options below for other setups.
+   - Set `builder.arch` to match the **server** (`amd64` or `arm64`). The default
+     is `amd64`, even if you deploy from an ARM laptop.
+
+   The local registry at `localhost:5555` runs on your deployment machine. Kamal
+   forwards it to the server over SSH while deploying, so you don't need a registry
+   account or a public registry port. The server doesn't need access to the fork.
+
+2. Create your local secrets file:
+
+   ```sh
+   cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   chmod 600 .kamal/secrets
+   bin/rails secret
+   ```
+
+   Paste the generated value into `SECRET_KEY_BASE` in `.kamal/secrets`, then fill
+   in `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`. Use your SMTP service's credentials,
+   not your Fizzy login. This file uses dotenv syntax; single-quote values that
+   contain characters such as `$` or `#` so they are treated literally.
+
+   Generate the secret key **once**, save it securely, and reuse it on every
+   deploy. No Rails encrypted credentials or `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` are required for
+   this setup. `.kamal/secrets` and its destination-specific variants are ignored
+   by Git, and `.kamal/` is excluded from Docker builds. Never put secrets in
+   `env.clear` or commit them to your fork.
+
+3. Commit your application and configuration changes. Kamal builds from the Git
+   checkout by default, so uncommitted application changes won't be deployed.
+   Do **not** commit the secrets file.
+
+## Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal builds the image from your fork's Dockerfile, starts the proxy, and boots
+Fizzy. The container entrypoint runs `db:prepare` before starting Rails, creating
+or migrating all four SQLite databases. Visit `https://your-hostname` to create
+your account. Check that you receive the sign-in email.
+
+For subsequent deployments, commit your changes and run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Useful commands:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs
+bin/kamal console
+bin/kamal shell
+```
+
+`bin/kamal config` can help diagnose configuration problems. Treat its output as
+**sensitive**: it may include secrets, so don't post it in issues or logs.
+
+## Mail options
+
+Set these non-secret values in `env.clear` in `config/deploy.yml` as needed:
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS` | Set in the deploy config | SMTP server hostname |
+| `SMTP_PORT` | `587` | SMTP server port |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN` | `APP_FQDN` | Domain sent in SMTP HELO/EHLO |
+| `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` | `plain` | Authentication method when a username is supplied, e.g. `plain` or `login` |
+| `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO` | `"true"` | Upgrade the SMTP connection with STARTTLS when supported |
+| `SMTP_TLS` | `"false"` | Use implicit TLS instead of STARTTLS (usually with port 465) |
+| `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` | Set in the deploy config | Verified sender, e.g. `Fizzy <fizzy@example.com>` |
+
+Keep booleans and ports quoted in the deploy config. For implicit TLS, set
+`SMTP_TLS: "true"` and `SMTP_PORT: "465"`; STARTTLS is then disabled automatically.
+For a trusted unauthenticated relay, leave `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`
+blank in `.kamal/secrets`; disable STARTTLS only if required by that relay. SMTP
+TLS is separate from the browser-facing HTTPS managed by Kamal Proxy.
+
+Browser push notifications are optional. To enable them, generate a VAPID key pair
+as described in the [development guide](development.md#web-push-notifications),
+put both keys in `.kamal/secrets`, and uncomment the two VAPID entries under
+`env.secret`. Keep these keys stable across deploys too.
+
+## Persistent data and backups
+
+The Docker named volume `fizzy_storage` is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It contains:
+
+- `production.sqlite3`: accounts, boards, cards, and other application data.
+- `production_queue.sqlite3`: background jobs and recurring task state.
+- `production_cache.sqlite3` and `production_cable.sqlite3`: cache and live updates.
+- `production/files`: uploads and stored exports.
+
+A new application container mounts the same volume on every deploy, including
+for migrations. Keep the volume name and mount unchanged. Docker initializes a
+new named volume with the image's storage directory ownership, allowing the
+non-root Rails user to write there. If you switch to a host bind mount, you must
+make that directory writable by UID/GID `1000:1000` yourself.
+
+Persistence is **not a backup**, and this setup isn't intended for multiple app
+servers sharing SQLite. Back up the entire volume and keep copies off the box.
+For a filesystem backup, stop the application (including its jobs) with
+`bin/kamal app stop`, snapshot/copy the volume, then use `bin/kamal app start`.
+Don't copy live SQLite files without a SQLite-aware backup tool or a consistent
+snapshot that includes their WAL files. Keep your secrets backed up separately.
+Never remove the volume or prune volumes containing your data.
+
+`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA` keeps background work on the same box without another role
+or container. The default two web workers and one job worker are a starting point;
+tune `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` for your server's memory and workload.
diff --git a/test/deployment_test.rb b/test/deployment_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d6fe4a9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deployment_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,169 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+require "open3"
+
+class DeploymentTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  test "Kamal deploys a single standalone container behind its TLS proxy" do
+    deployment = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"), version: "test")
+
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], deployment.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal 1, deployment.all_hosts.size
+    assert_empty deployment.accessories
+    assert deployment.proxy.ssl?
+    assert_equal 80, deployment.proxy.app_port
+    assert_equal [ deployment.primary_host ], deployment.proxy.hosts
+    assert deployment.registry.local?
+    assert_equal "/rails/public/assets", deployment.asset_path
+    assert_includes deployment.volume_args, "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+
+    env = deployment.env.clear
+    assert_equal deployment.proxy.hosts.first, env.fetch("APP_FQDN")
+    assert_equal "false", env.fetch("SAAS")
+    assert_equal "production", env.fetch("RAILS_ENV")
+    assert_equal "sqlite", env.fetch("DATABASE_ADAPTER")
+    assert_equal "local", env.fetch("ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE")
+    assert_equal "true", env.fetch("SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA")
+
+    %w[ SECRET_KEY_BASE SMTP_USERNAME SMTP_PASSWORD ].each do |secret|
+      assert_includes deployment.env.secret_keys, secret
+      refute_includes env.keys, secret
+    end
+  end
+
+  test "production uses the deployment hostname and authenticated SMTP" do
+    configuration = production_configuration
+
+    assert_equal "https://fizzy.example.test/", configuration.fetch("root_url")
+    assert_equal "fizzy.example.test", configuration.dig("mailer_url_options", "host")
+    assert_equal "https", configuration.dig("mailer_url_options", "protocol")
+    assert_equal "fizzy.example.test", configuration.dig("controller_url_options", "host")
+    assert_equal "smtp", configuration.fetch("delivery_method")
+    assert_equal "Fizzy <fizzy@example.test>", configuration.fetch("from")
+
+    smtp = configuration.fetch("smtp")
+    assert_equal "smtp.example.test", smtp.fetch("address")
+    assert_equal 587, smtp.fetch("port")
+    assert_equal "fizzy.example.test", smtp.fetch("domain")
+    assert_equal "test-user", smtp.fetch("user_name")
+    assert_equal "test-password", smtp.fetch("password")
+    assert_equal "plain", smtp.fetch("authentication")
+    assert_equal true, smtp.fetch("enable_starttls_auto")
+    assert_equal true, configuration.fetch("assume_ssl")
+    assert_equal true, configuration.fetch("force_ssl")
+  end
+
+  test "production keeps databases and uploads in the persistent storage directory" do
+    configuration = production_configuration
+
+    assert_equal %w[ cable cache primary queue ], configuration.fetch("databases").keys.sort
+    configuration.fetch("databases").each_value do |database|
+      assert_equal "sqlite3", database.fetch("adapter")
+      assert database.fetch("database").start_with?("storage/")
+    end
+    assert_equal Rails.root.join("storage/production/files").to_s, configuration.fetch("storage_root")
+    assert_equal "solid_queue", configuration.fetch("queue_adapter")
+  end
+
+  test "the secrets example supplies every required deployment secret" do
+    raw_config = Kamal::Configuration.load_raw_config(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+    deployment = Kamal::Configuration.new(raw_config.merge(secrets_path: Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets.example").to_s), version: "test")
+
+    assert_equal deployment.env.secret_keys.sort, deployment.secrets.to_h.keys.sort
+    assert deployment.env.secret_keys.all? { |key| deployment.env.to_h.key?(key) }
+  end
+
+  test "implicit SMTP TLS disables STARTTLS" do
+    configuration = production_configuration("SMTP_PORT" => "465", "SMTP_TLS" => "true", "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => "true")
+
+    assert_equal 465, configuration.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_equal true, configuration.dig("smtp", "tls")
+    assert_equal false, configuration.dig("smtp", "enable_starttls_auto")
+  end
+
+  test "SMTP supports a trusted relay without credentials" do
+    configuration = production_configuration(
+      "SMTP_PORT" => "25", "SMTP_DOMAIN" => "mail.example.test", "SMTP_USERNAME" => "",
+      "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "", "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => "plain", "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => "false"
+    )
+
+    assert_equal 25, configuration.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_equal "mail.example.test", configuration.dig("smtp", "domain")
+    assert_nil configuration.dig("smtp", "user_name")
+    assert_nil configuration.dig("smtp", "password")
+    assert_nil configuration.dig("smtp", "authentication")
+    assert_equal false, configuration.dig("smtp", "enable_starttls_auto")
+  end
+
+  test "production can boot for asset builds without deployment settings" do
+    configuration = production_configuration(
+      "APP_FQDN" => nil, "SMTP_ADDRESS" => nil, "SECRET_KEY_BASE" => nil, "SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY" => "1"
+    )
+
+    assert_nil configuration.fetch("root_url")
+    assert_empty configuration.fetch("mailer_url_options")
+    assert_equal "localhost", configuration.dig("smtp", "address")
+  end
+
+  test "generated URLs use HTTPS behind a proxy even when redirects are disabled" do
+    configuration = production_configuration("FORCE_SSL" => "false", "ASSUME_SSL" => "true")
+
+    assert_equal "https://fizzy.example.test/", configuration.fetch("root_url")
+    assert_equal "https", configuration.dig("mailer_url_options", "protocol")
+    assert_equal false, configuration.fetch("force_ssl")
+  end
+
+  test "generated URLs honor the existing SSL opt out" do
+    configuration = production_configuration("DISABLE_SSL" => "true")
+
+    assert_equal "http://fizzy.example.test/", configuration.fetch("root_url")
+    assert_equal "http", configuration.dig("mailer_url_options", "protocol")
+    assert_equal false, configuration.fetch("assume_ssl")
+    assert_equal false, configuration.fetch("force_ssl")
+  end
+
+  private
+    def production_configuration(overrides = {})
+      env = {
+        "SAAS" => "false",
+        "BUNDLE_GEMFILE" => Rails.root.join("Gemfile").to_s,
+        "RAILS_ENV" => "production",
+        "SECRET_KEY_BASE" => SecureRandom.hex(64),
+        "SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY" => nil,
+        "DATABASE_ADAPTER" => "sqlite",
+        "DATABASE_URL" => nil,
+        "ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE" => "local",
+        "APP_FQDN" => "fizzy.example.test",
+        "MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS" => "Fizzy <fizzy@example.test>",
+        "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.test",
+        "SMTP_PORT" => nil,
+        "SMTP_DOMAIN" => nil,
+        "SMTP_USERNAME" => "test-user",
+        "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "test-password",
+        "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => nil,
+        "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => nil,
+        "SMTP_TLS" => nil,
+        "DISABLE_SSL" => nil,
+        "ASSUME_SSL" => nil,
+        "FORCE_SSL" => nil
+      }.merge(overrides)
+
+      output, errors, status = Open3.capture3(env, Rails.root.join("bin/rails").to_s, "runner", <<~RUBY)
+        puts JSON.generate(
+          root_url: Rails.application.routes.default_url_options[:host] && Rails.application.routes.url_helpers.root_url,
+          mailer_url_options: ActionMailer::Base.default_url_options,
+          controller_url_options: ActionController::Base.default_url_options,
+          delivery_method: ActionMailer::Base.delivery_method,
+          from: ApplicationMailer.default[:from],
+          smtp: ActionMailer::Base.smtp_settings,
+          assume_ssl: Rails.application.config.assume_ssl,
+          force_ssl: Rails.application.config.force_ssl,
+          databases: ActiveRecord::Base.configurations.configs_for(env_name: "production").to_h { |db| [ db.name, { adapter: db.adapter, database: db.database } ] },
+          storage_root: ActiveStorage::Blob.service.root.to_s,
+          queue_adapter: ActiveJob::Base.queue_adapter_name
+        )
+      RUBY
+
+      assert status.success?, "Production boot failed:\n#{output}\n#{errors}"
+      JSON.parse(output.lines.last)
+    end
+end
